WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.236
Biomedical ceramic design starts with a difficult

00:00:04.236 --> 00:00:04.763
demand:

00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:08.311
a manufactured structure must remain useful in a

00:00:08.311 --> 00:00:10.011
biological environment.

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:17.456
Additive manufacturing can produce complex ceramic

00:00:17.456 --> 00:00:17.890
forms.

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:21.216
Processing, internal architecture and material

00:00:21.216 --> 00:00:21.939
properties

00:00:21.939 --> 00:00:24.070
shape what those forms can do.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:31.168
Ceramic processing and densification impose

00:00:31.168 --> 00:00:31.944
constraints

00:00:31.944 --> 00:00:35.049
beyond the original digital model. The final

00:00:35.049 --> 00:00:35.755
properties

00:00:35.755 --> 00:00:36.933
must be measured.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.231
Mechanical performance and biological response can

00:00:44.231 --> 00:00:44.525
pull

00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:48.423
design in different directions. An elegant lattice is

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:48.644
not

00:00:48.644 --> 00:00:50.885
automatically a medical device.

00:00:54.925 --> 00:00:58.748
Clinical application also depends on repeatability,

00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:02.646
sterilisation and regulatory pathways; each requires

00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:05.345
evidence distinct from printability.

00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:12.792
The accepted chapter reviews biomedical ceramic

00:01:12.792 --> 00:01:16.564
applications and translational constraints. It is an

00:01:16.564 --> 00:01:20.335
editorial explanation, not a new implantation trial.
